// Permit commands, but refuse one dangerous line.
//
// A shell permit does not bound what a command touches — every file each command reads
// or writes raises its own `fs:*` decision, and the filesystem rules are what confine
// the agent. Pair this file with the project read and write rules from an agent example.
//
// `shell:exec` and `shell:spawn` are two actions with no group between them. This file
// permits `shell:exec` only. `shell:spawn` authorizes a HOST binary that reaches the
// kernel rather than the shell's virtual filesystem, so no `fs:*` decision is raised
// for what it does; leave it absent unless you mean to grant that.

@id("shell_commands")
permit (principal, action == Box::Action::"shell:exec", resource);

// Refuse `rm -rf`. `program` is what the first word resolved to, not how it was spelled.
// `arg1` is optional, so a rule that reads it must guard it with `has`; an unguarded
// read is a load error, not a rule that quietly matches nothing.
@id("no_rm_rf")
@description("`rm -rf` is refused. Remove files one path at a time, or ask the operator to permit the recursive form.")
forbid (principal, action == Box::Action::"shell:exec", resource)
when {
  context.input.program == "rm" &&
  context.input has arg1 && context.input.arg1 == "-rf"
};
